digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56522-5
  rankdir="TB";
  "I1" [label="(0,0,0) If a method is verified feasible and its core component is adaptable, it can be generalized for diverse inverse problems."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes and evaluates a Deep Empirical Neural Network that combines a deep-image-prior-style convnet with an experimentally calibrated differentiable transmission matrix for single-shot optical phase retrieval through scattering media such as multimode fibers and ground glass, aiming to work without analytical models, labeled data, or prior training and to improve reconstruction fidelity relative to supervised approaches while acknowledging inference-time tradeoffs.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Together, a data-free untrained-network motivation, empirical transmission-matrix integration, and bounded demonstrations through scattering media form the methodological basis for evaluating DENN as an optical phase-retrieval approach."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Supervised learning and unsupervised/self-supervised learning approaches commonly still depend on large amounts of data, so data requirements remain a central limitation for learning-based inverse-problem methods."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) In the reported DENN, an experimentally calibrated empirical transmission matrix is integrated with the neural network to provide update information for back propagation."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) For a calibrated scattering system, integrating an empirical transmission matrix with the neural network can provide a forward mapping relationship between inputs and outputs for DENN optimization."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The DENN approach eliminates the need for labeled data and prior training by integrating an empirical model with a convnet, and it is demonstrated in single-shot seeing-through tasks for a multimode fiber and a ground glass."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) In the reported comparison, supervised learning requires about 26 hours of training and 0.48 seconds of inference, whereas DENN performs optical phase retrieval with 270 seconds of inference and more than 58% higher fidelity."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The demonstrations support DENN feasibility without labeled data or prior training and show a reconstruction-fidelity advantage over supervised learning in the reported comparison, while also indicating an inference-time tradeoff."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) The evidence supports DENN feasibility in the demonstrated settings and supports the role of an empirical transmission matrix as a calibrated forward-mapping component for the scattering system."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The demonstrated DENN feasibility and calibrated empirical forward mapping motivate investigating whether DENN can be extended to diverse inverse problems beyond the tested scattering systems."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because many learning approaches still require large data while untrained deep-image-prior-inspired networks are already used in imaging-related fields, these facts motivate the pursuit of untrained deep neural networks."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Untrained deep-image-prior-inspired neural networks are relevant to optical metrology, holography, imaging, and physics-enhanced phase-retrieval contexts, but the premises do not by themselves prove a specific catalog of DIP variants for diffraction optics."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Physics-enhanced neural-network approaches to phase retrieval can use loss functions based on analytical theory when the phase retardation or forward physics is precisely described by such a theory."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Analytical-theory-based physics-enhanced phase-retrieval approaches are limited for optical systems that lack an analytical theory, including scattering systems with non-negligible backward scattering."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) For scattering systems where analytical-theory-based approaches are limited, an empirical model that provides experimental update information is a plausible basis for developing a DENN approach."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Physics-enhanced neural-network approaches that depend on analytical-theory-based losses are not directly applicable to optical scattering systems that lack an analytical theory."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Applying deep-image-prior-inspired untrained neural networks to optical phase retrieval through scattering media is motivated by their imaging use but remains challenging when the scattering system lacks an analytical theory."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The challenge of applying DIP-inspired untrained networks to scattering media, together with the promise of empirical update information, motivates reporting a Deep Empirical Neural Network for scattering systems without analytical theory."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) If the phase retardation can be precisely described by a theory, then physics-enhanced deep neural networks are quite efficient in dealing with the ill-posed problem of phase retrieval."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) There are many optical systems without analytical theory, where wave scattering systems with non-negligible backward scattering are typical examples."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) If an empirical model captures the relationship between multiple input and their corresponding output for the scattering system, then it describes the forward mapping relationship."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Unlike an analytical theory, the empirical model resembles a partial experimental measurement of the system, providing insights for the update of DENN."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Supervised learning excels at learning a complex relationship from large amounts of input and output data pairs."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) The need of labeled data and training for the DENN can be eliminated by integrating the empirical model of the opaque system with a convnet."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) Seeing through a multimode fiber (MMF) and a ground glass are achieved using a single-shot speckle intensity at their output."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Supervised learning using 30000 data pairs takes about 26 hours for training and 0.48 second for inference."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) The DENN achieve the same goal of optical phase retrieval using the inference time of 270 seconds, all while delivering over 58% improvement in fidelity."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Unsupervised and self-supervised learning have been proposed to release the heavy effort of registering data pairs, while sufficiently large amounts of data are still need."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Untrained neural networks, inspired by deep image prior concept, have been widely used in metrology, holography, and imaging."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) The weights and biases of a physics-enhanced deep neural network can be updated using a loss function based on the analytical theory."];
  "S2" -> "R1" [label="induction-case"];
  "S4" -> "R1" [label="induction-common"];
  "R1" -> "R2" [label="induction-case"];
